===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The state of being [[free]].
#: {{syn|en|freehood<q:rare or nonstandard>}}
#: {{ux|en|Having recently been released from prison, he didn't know what to do with his newfound '''freedom'''.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=w:Richard Nixon|title=Richard Nixon on ‘Inside Washington’|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171009010438/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o2mhvEuGHLI|archivedate=09 October 2017|network=w:Seoul Broadcasting System|publisher=w:Richard Nixon Foundation|url2=https://www.nixonfoundation.org/1992/03/richard-nixon-on-inside-washington/|date=March 30, 1992|time=13:46|passage=Well Russia at the present time is at a crossroads. It is often said that the Cold War is over and that the West has won it- that's only half true. Because what has happened is that the communists have been defeated, but the ideas of '''freedom''' now are on trial. If they don't work, there will be a reversion to, not communism which has failed, but what I call a new despotism which would pose a mortal danger to the rest of the world because it would be infected with the virus of Russian imperialism which of course has been a characteristic of Russian foreign policy for centuries.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|title=The internet is in decline – it needs rewilding
|author=John Naughton
|date=2024-05-04
|url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/article/2024/may/04/the-internet-is-in-decline-it-needs-rewilding
|passage=However, the next generation of innovators to benefit from this '''freedom''' – Google, Facebook, Amazon, Microsoft, Apple et al – saw no reason to extend it to anyone else.|archiveurl=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/fKcre|archivedate=5 May 2024}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} The [[lack]] of a specific [[constraint]], or of constraints in general; a state of being free, [[unconstrained]].
#: {{syn|en|freeness}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Freedom''' of speech is a basic democratic value.}}
#: {{ux|en|People in our city enjoy many '''freedoms'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Every child has a right to '''freedom''' from fear and '''freedom''' from want.}}
#* {{quote-av|en
|year=1984
|role=w:Princess Irulan
|title={{w|Dune (1984 film)|Dune}}
|url=https://archive.org/details/Dune19843640x272435mb
|genre=[[science fiction|Science Fiction]]
|oclc=1295459964
|time=1:50
|text=The spice exists on only one planet in the entire universe- a desolate, dry planet with vast deserts. Hidden away within the rocks of these deserts are a people known as the {{w|Fremen}}, who have long held a prophecy, that a man would come, a messiah, who would lead them to true '''freedom'''. The planet is Arrakis, also known as Dune.}}
#* {{quote-book|en
|year=2010
|author=w:George W. Bush
|title=w:Decision Points
|url=https://archive.org/details/decisionpointsgeorge0000bush_v6x8/
|ISBN=978-0-307-59061-9
|OCLC=1089496474
|OL=24416755M
|page=23
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/decisionpointsgeorge0000bush_v6x8/page/22/
|text=China's experience reminded me of the French and Russian revolutions. The pattern was the same: People seized control by promising to promote certain ideals. Once they had consolidated power, they abused it, casting aside their beliefs and brutalizing their fellow citizens. It was as if mankind had a sickness that it kept inflicting on itself. The sobering thought deepened my conviction that '''freedom'''— economic, political, and religious — is the only fair and productive way of governing a society.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-07|author=w:Gary Younge
|volume=188|issue=26|page=18|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/2013/jun/02/hypocrisy-lies-at-heart-bradley-manning-trial Hypocrisy lies at heart of Manning prosecution]
|passage=The dispatches [&hellip;] also exposed the blatant discrepancy between the west's professed values and actual foreign policies. Having lectured the Arab world about democracy for years, its collusion in suppressing '''freedom''' was undeniable as protesters were met by weaponry and tear gas made in the west, employed by a military trained by westerners.}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times
|date=August 4, 2021
|author=Hahna Yoon
|title=Tracing Freedom to a Pair of Jeans
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210804090224/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/04/style/north-korean-defector-fashion.html
|archivedate=04 August 2021
|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/04/style/north-korean-defector-fashion.html
|text=“When I lived in North Korea, I never had the '''freedom''' to wear what I wanted, but I never questioned it because I didn’t know this '''freedom''' existed,” said Jihyun Kang, 31, who grew up in Chongjin, the third-largest city in North Korea.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2026 April 1|author=Christian Wolmar|title=Managers must be given '''freedom''' to make decisions|journal=RAIL|issue=1058|page=35|text=[Chris] Green asked for the same '''freedoms''' he had been given in Scotland, and Reid and Transport Secretary Nicholas Ridley agreed.}} 
# The right or privilege of unrestricted use or access
#: {{ux|en|'''Freedom''' of a city}}
#* {{RQ:Gibbon Roman Empire|I|2i|passage=Yet the wisest princes, who adopted the maxims of Augustus, guarded with the strictest care the dignity of the Roman name, and diffused the '''freedom''' of the city with a prudent liberality.}}
#* '''1854''', ''History, Gazetteer, and Directory, of Norfolk, and the City and County of the City of Norwich ...'' (Sheffield, Francis White & Co.), p 77:
#*: The '''freedom''' of the city can now only be acquired by birth or servitude; but many were formerly admitted by gift and purchase, a fine of from £ 3 to £ 25, according to trade, being charged on the admission of strangers.
# Frankness; openness; unreservedness.
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|letter=50|text=I doubt not, that you will take amiss my '''freedom'''; but as you have deserved it from me, I shall be less and less concerned on that score, as I see you are more and more intent to show your wit at the expense of justice and compassion.}}
# Improper familiarity; violation of the rules of [[decorum]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1828|author=James Hogg|title=Mary Burnet
|passage="A first love is not easily extinguished, Mr. Allanson," said she. "You may guess from my appearance, that I have been fortunate in life; but, for all that, my first love for you has continued the same, unaltered and unchanged, and you must forgive the little '''freedoms''' I used to-day to try your affections, and the effects my appearance would have on you."}}

====Usage notes====
* ''Freedom from'' can be followed by various nouns, typically, ''fear, want, hunger, pain, hatred, disease, stress, depression, debt, poverty, necessity, violence, war, advertising, addiction'', etc.

