pages.extract:
In chemical thermodynamics, the reaction quotient (




          Q


              r





    {\displaystyle Q_{\mathrm {r} }}

 or just 



        Q


    {\displaystyle Q}

) is a dimensionless quantity that provides a measurement of the relative amounts of products and reactants present in a reaction mixture for a reaction with well-defined overall stoichiometry at a particular point in time.  Mathematically, it is defined as the ratio of the activities (or molar concentrations) of the product species over those of the reactant species involved in the chemical reaction, taking stoichiometric coefficients of the reaction into account as exponents of the concentrations.

main.content:
==English==

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:af|a-<id:on>|float|text=+|tree=1}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈfloʊt/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-afloat.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈfləʊt/|a=RP}}
* {{enPR|ə-flōtˈ}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊt|s=2}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# In or into a state of [[float#Verb|float]]ing.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1668|author=w:John Dryden|title=w:Sir Martin Mar-all|location=London|publisher=H. Herringman|section=act II|page=22|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A36685.0001.001
|passage=You have so little Brains, that a [[pennyworth|Penn’orth]] of Butter melted under ’um, would set ’um '''afloat''':}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Robinson Crusoe|page=224|text={{...}} I went down to my Boat, got the Water out of her, and got her '''afloat''', loaded all my Cargo in her, and then went Home again for more;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1881|author=w:Christina Rossetti|chapter=De Profundis|title=A Pageant and Other Poems|url=https://archive.org/details/pageantotherpoem00rossrich/page/60/mode/1up?q=afloat|page=60|publisher=Macmillan|location=London
|passage=Oh why is heaven built so far, / Oh why is earth set so remote? / I cannot reach the nearest star / That hangs '''afloat'''.}}
# In, or while in, a [[vessel]] at [[sea]] or on another [[body of water]]; [[at sea]].
#: {{ant|en|ashore}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1788|author={{w|Alexander Jardine (British Army officer)|Alexander Jardine}}|title=Letters from Barbary, France, Spain, Portugal, &c.|location=London|publisher=T. Cadell|section=Volume 2, Letter 23, p. 236|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004854575.0001.002
|passage={{...}} that trade {{...}} may likewise employ many useful hands both ashore and '''afloat''',}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Treasure Island|11|page=88|text=They was the roughest crew '''afloat''', was Flint’s; the devil himself would have been feared to go to sea with them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1947|author=w:James Michener|title=w:Tales of the South Pacific|location=New York|publisher=Dial|year_published=2014|chapter=The Strike|page=315|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=yQBwDwAAQBAJ&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=Navy chow ashore is rarely as good as it is '''afloat''', and for enlisted men it is usually much worse.}}
# Under water (bearing floating objects).
#: {{syn|en|awash}}
#* '''1695''', {{w|Edmund Gibson}} (translator), ''{{w|William Camden|Camden’s}} Britannia,'' London: A. Swalle, “Staffordshire,”<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/B18452.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} it [the {{w|River Dove, Central England|River Dove}}] overflows and lays the meadows '''afloat''' in April, like another Nile.
# {{lb|en|figurative|of ideas, information, etc.}} In or into [[circulation]] or [[currency]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1587|author=w:Raphael Holinshed; et al|title={{w|Holinshed's Chronicles|Holinshed’s Chronicles}}|volume=3|publisher={{w|Edward I of England|Edward I}}|page=298|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A68202.0001.001
|passage=setting a lie '''aflote'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1878|author=w:Thomas Hardy|title=w:The Return of the Native|location=London|publisher=Smith, Elder|volume=2|chapter=5|page=163|url=https://archive.org/details/returnofnative02hard/page/163/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage={{...}} I shall not be judged fairly; it will get '''afloat''' that I am not a good girl,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1757|author=w:William Burke; w:Edmund Burke|title=An Account of the European Settlements in America|location=London|publisher=R. and J. Dodsley|section=Volume 2, Part 7, Chapter 4, p. 150|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004909745.0001.002
|passage={{...}} as this example set the discourse about witchcraft '''afloat''', some people, troubled with a similar complaint, began to fancy themselves bewitched too.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|figurative|of an emotional state}} In or into a condition of [[stimulation]], [[arousal]], [[confusion]], [[bewilderment]], etc.
#: {{syn|en|at sea}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1821|author=w:William Hazlitt|title=w:Table-Talk|location=London|publisher=John Warren, Essay 1|page=3|url=https://archive.org/details/tabletalkororigi00hazluoft/page/n14/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage=No angry passions rise to disturb the silent progress of the work, {{...}} no irritable humours are set '''afloat''':}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1878|author=w:John Berwick Harwood|title=Helena Lady Harrogate,|location=London|publisher=Richard Bentley|volume=1|chapter=17|page=312|url=https://archive.org/details/helenaladyharro01harwgoog/page/n325/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage={{...}} they knew how to abstain from the overdose of liquor that sets the brain '''afloat''' and loosens the tongue.}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# [[float|Floating]].
#: {{ux|en|A rubber duck and other toys were '''afloat''' in the bath.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|IV|3|text=On such a full sea are we now '''afloat'''; / And we must take the current when it serves, / Or lose our ventures.}}
# In, or found while in, a [[vessel]] at [[sea]] or on another [[body of water]].
#: {{ant|en|ashore}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1788|author={{w|Alexander Jardine (British Army officer)|Alexander Jardine}}|title=Letters from Barbary, France, Spain, Portugal, &c.|location=London|publisher=T. Cadell|section=Volume 2, Letter 23, p. 236|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004854575.0001.002
|passage={{...}} that trade {{...}} may likewise employ many useful hands both ashore and '''afloat''',}}
# Floating in the [[air#Noun|air]]; [[flow#Verb|flow]]ing freely; not tied, [[braid]]ed, etc. {{q|of hair or clothing}}
#* {{RQ:Cleland Fanny Hill|1|text=her black hair loose and '''a-float''' down her dazzling white neck}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=w:Poul Anderson|title=w:The Broken Sword|url=https://archive.org/details/brokensword00poul/page/11/mode/1up?q=afloat|chapter=4|page=11|publisher=Ballantine Books|year_published=1981|location=New York
|passage=unbound silvery-gold tresses '''afloat''' beneath a jeweled coronet}}
#* {{RQ:Smith White Teeth|page=152|passage={{...}} she roars down the street, [[dreadlocks|dreads]] and feathers and cape '''afloat''',}}
# Covered with water, bearing floating objects.
#: {{syn|en|awash|flooded}}
#: {{ux|en|The decks are '''afloat'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1938|author=w:Marjorie Kinnan Rawlings|title=w:The Yearling|url=https://archive.org/details/yearling00rawl/page/233/mode/1up?q=afloat|chapter=19|page=233|publisher=Scribner|location=New York
|passage=The yard was '''afloat'''. Jody looked out of the window and saw two drowned biddies floating about with upturned bellies.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[cover|Covered]], [[overspread]], [[filled]] (''with'' or ''in'' something).
#: {{syn|en|alive|awash}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911|author=w:D. H. Lawrence|title=w:The White Peacock|location=London|publisher=Heinemann|section=Part 2, Chapter 2, p. 233|url=https://archive.org/details/whitepeacock00lawr/page/233/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage=The larch-wood was '''afloat''' with clear, lyric green,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=w:Lucy Maud Montgomery|title=w:Mistress Pat|location=Sydney|publisher=Angus & Robertson|year_published=1937|page=100|url=https://archive.org/details/mistress-pat-by-montgomery/page/100/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage=The world was '''afloat''' in primrose light, pale and exquisite.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1979|author=w:Bernard Malamud|title={{w|Dubin's Lives|Dubin’s Lives}}|location=New York|publisher=Farrar Straus Giroux|section=Part 1, p. 51|url=https://archive.org/details/dubinslives00mala/page/51/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage=The lobby was '''afloat''' with men, single and married, meeting pretty women in bright dresses and pants suits, single and married.}}
# {{lb|en|of an organization}} Having just enough resources to continue to [[operate]]; barely able to pay [[expense]]s; {{lb|en|of a private individual, family, etc.}} [[keep one's head above water|keeping one's head above water]].
#: {{syn|en|on one's feet|solvent}}
#: {{ant|en|bankrupt|insolvent}}
#: {{ux|en|The donation will keep our business '''afloat''' for quite a while.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1549|translator=w:Miles Coverdale|title=The Paraphrase of {{w|Erasmus}} upon the New Testament|location=London|publisher=Edward Whitchurche|section=Volume 2, {{w|Epistle to the Philippians|Philippians}} 4|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A68942.0001.001
|passage={{...}} you nede not to be sorye, as thoughe your frendely liberalitie had not be very acceptable vnto me. I haue receaued euery thing, and now I am '''afloate''', by your lyberall sendyng.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1753|author=w:Tobias Smollett|title=w:The Adventures of Ferdinand, Count Fathom|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Mundell & Son|year_published=1800|chapter=54|pages=306–307|url=https://archive.org/details/miscellaneouswor04smoliala/page/307/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage=He {{...}} endeavoured, by forcing himself into a lower path of life than any he had hitherto trod, to keep himself '''afloat''', with the portion of some tradesman’s daughter, whom he meant to espouse.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Vanity Fair|34|page=309|text={{...}} the price poor Jos Osborne had paid for her two horses was in itself sufficient to keep their little establishment '''afloat''' for a year, at least;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=Harriet W. Daly|title=Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia|page=191|passage=They somehow manage to keep "'''afloat'''," so as to obtain the needful funds to pay their passages and to purchase, tools and rations.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=w:Nadifa Mohamed|title=w:Black Mamba Boy|url=https://archive.org/details/blackmambaboy00moha/page/67/mode/1up?q=afloat|page=67|publisher=Farrar, Straus and Giroux|location=New York
|passage=The clan handouts that kept other Somalis '''afloat''' were absent here, as the Yibros were so few and so poor.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Fredreka Schouten and Arit John|title=The DNC’s cash crunch deepens as new filings show Republicans with a huge advantage|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/06/21/politics/dnc-financial-troubles-fec-filings|date=2025-06-21|passage=The party’s cash crunch is serious enough that party officials have weighed seeking a line of credit to help it stay '''afloat''', a development first reported by The New York Times.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250816035510/https://www.cnn.com/2025/06/21/politics/dnc-financial-troubles-fec-filings|archivedate=16 August 2025}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|of ideas, information, etc.}} Believed or talked about by many people; being passed from person to person.
#: {{syn|en|circulating|in circulation|current}}
#: {{ux|en|The supervisor was never fired, though countless accusations of dishonesty were '''afloat'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1945|author=w:Evelyn Waugh|title=w:Brideshead Revisited|location=London|publisher=Chapman & Hall|section=Book 2, Chapter 3, p. 243|url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.208486/page/n245/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage={{...}} she and I were accepted, whatever ugly rumours had been '''afloat''' in the past year, as man and wife.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|figurative|of an emotional state}} [[stimulate|Stimulated]], [[arouse]]d, [[activate]]d.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1769|author=w:Elizabeth Griffith|title=w:The School for Rakes|location=London|publisher=T. Becket and P.A. De Hondt, Epilogue|page=92|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004795556.0001.000
|passage=You’ll find, when once my passion is '''afloat''', / The soul of ''Caesar,'' in a petticoat!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1794|author=w:Thomas Holcroft|title=The Adventures of Hugh Trevor|location=London|publisher=Shepperson and Reynolds|volume=1|chapter=6|page=46|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004841298.0001.001
|passage=My half frozen blood and my fears again '''afloat''' made me tremble through every limb;}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|figurative}} In a state of [[confusion]], [[bewilderment]], or [[distraction]].
#: {{syn|en|at sea|bewildered|confused|distracted}}
#* '''1789''', {{w|Edward Gibbon}}, letter to {{w|John Baker Holroyd, 1st Earl of Sheffield|Lord Sheffield}} dated August 1789, in ''Miscellaneous Works,'' London: A. Strahan ''[[et al.]],'' 1796, p.{{nbsp}}201,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004849601.0001.001]</sup>
#*: I know not what to say; my mind is all '''afloat'''; yet you will not reproach me with caprice or inconstancy.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=w:Harry Castlemon|title=Our Fellows|location=Philadelphia|publisher=John C. Winston|chapter=1|page=10|url=https://archive.org/details/ourfellowsorskir00cast/page/9/mode/1up?q=afloat
|passage={{...}} he could correctly analyze and parse any sentence you could give him, no matter how complex; but when it came to talking he was all '''afloat'''.}}

====Usage notes====
As an adjective, '''afloat''' can be used only [[postpositive]]ly: ''The cardboard ships were '''afloat''' in the bathtub'', or ''The cardboard ships, '''afloat''' in the bathtub, were soon waterlogged''; but not *''The '''afloat''' toys soon sank because they were made of cardboard.'' In that context, ''floating'' can be used instead.

